Isolate a triangle, with base length $200$ (a side of the octagon). This triangle is obviously isoceles. Denote the other side length as $x$. Since the interior angle is $45$ degrees (due to the shape being an octagon), then we can apply Law of Cosines to this triangle. We get: 
\begin{eqnarray*} 200^2 &=& 2x^2 - 2x^2*cos(45^\circ) \\ &=& 2x^2 - 2x^2*\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \\ &=& (2-\sqrt{2})x^2 \end{eqnarray*}
And thus \[x = \frac{200}{\sqrt{2-\sqrt{2}}}\]
From the above, $x = 20\sqrt{r}$, so we get
\begin{eqnarray*} r &=& (\frac{200}{20(\sqrt{2-\sqrt{2}})})^2 \\ &=& (\frac{10}{\sqrt{2-\sqrt{2}})})^2 \cdot \frac{2+\sqrt{2}}{2+\sqrt{2}} \\ &=& \frac{200 + 100\sqrt{2}}{2} \\ &=& 100 + 50\sqrt{2} \end{eqnarray*}
And hence the answer is $100 + 50 + 2 \Rightarrow \boxed{152}$

