We apply the Change of Base Formula, then rearrange:
\begin{align*} \frac{\log_2{4}}{\log_2{(3x)}}&=\frac{\log_2{8}}{\log_2{(2x)}} \\ \frac{2}{\log_2{(3x)}}&=\frac{3}{\log_2{(2x)}} \\ 3\log_2{(3x)}&=2\log_2{(2x)}. \\ \end{align*}
By the logarithmic identity $n\log_b{a}=\log_b{\left(a^n\right)},$ it follows that
\begin{align*} \log_2{\left[(3x)^3\right]}&=\log_2{\left[(2x)^2\right]} \\ (3x)^3&=(2x)^2 \\ 27x^3&=4x^2 \\ x&=\frac{4}{27}, \end{align*}
from which the answer is $4+27=\boxed{31}.$

