\begin{align*} (3x)^y&=4, \\ (2x)^y&=8. \end{align*}
Cubing the first equation and squaring the second equation, we have
\begin{align*} (3x)^{3y}&=64, \\ (2x)^{2y}&=64. \end{align*}
Applying the Transitive Property, we get 
\begin{align*} (3x)^{3y}&=(2x)^{2y} \\ (3x)^3&=(2x)^2 \\ 27x^3&=4x^2 \\ x&=\frac{4}{27}, \end{align*}
from which the answer is $4+27=\boxed{31}.$

