We know that, as the answer is an integer, $n$ must be some power of $16.$ Testing $16$ yields
\begin{align*} \log_2{(\log_{16}{16})} &= \log_4{(\log_4{16})} \\ \log_2{1} &= \log_4{2} \\ 0 &= \frac{1}{2}, \end{align*}
which does not work. We then try $256,$ giving us
\begin{align*} \log_2{(\log_{16}{256})} &= \log_4{(\log_4{256})} \\ \log_2{2} &= \log_4{4} \\ 1 &= 1, \end{align*}
which holds true. Thus, $n = 256,$ so the answer is $2 + 5 + 6 = \boxed{13}.$
